@startuml
' Figure 5.9 – System sequence diagram: live notification delivery over SSE
!include ../_style.puml
mainframe interaction SequenceDiagram Notification delivery
autonumber "0 :"

participant "Reader" as U
participant "System" as A
participant "Database" as D
participant "Writer's\nbrowser" as W

group Subscription, once per session
  W -> A : GET /notifications/stream
  activate A
  A -> A : Register the writer in the subscriber map
  A --> W : 200, text/event-stream, unbuffered by nginx
  loop every 30 seconds
    A --> W : Heartbeat comment
  end
  note right of A
    The heartbeat exists because nginx closes an idle
    proxied connection after 60 seconds. Without it the
    stream dies quietly and the bell stops updating.
  end note
end

|||

U -> A : Like the writer's article
group One database transaction
  A -> D : Insert the like
  activate D
  A -> D : Insert the notification row
  D --> A : Committed
  deactivate D
end
A -> A : Only now, after the commit,\npush to the subscriber map
note right of A
  Emitting inside the transaction would announce
  something a rollback then un-did.
end note
A --> W : Server-sent event with an id
W --> W : Increment the unread badge, prepend to the dropdown
A --> U : 201
deactivate A

|||

group Reconnection
  W -> W : The connection drops
  W -> A : GET /notifications/stream with Last-Event-ID
  activate A
  A -> D : Read the notifications newer than that id
  activate D
  D --> A : Missed rows
  deactivate D
  A --> W : Replay, then resume streaming
  deactivate A
end

note over U, W
  Delivery is best-effort; the database is the record. The
  bell reads the persisted list on load, so a notification
  missed while offline is never lost, only delayed.
end note
@enduml
